-- S-instantiated-reselect (#1909), the ACCEPT shape, DECLARED channel.
--
-- Hand-derived from DICT-SEMANTICS §3 (`assum` before `inst`, and selection is
-- never a function of declaration order) BEFORE running anything.  `nest`'s
-- declared context is `Tag (Wrap a)` -- ONE predicate whose argument is
-- STRUCTURED, not the bare `Tag a` the compiler's own constraints all are.  Each
-- CALL instantiates it: `nest True` poses `Tag (Wrap Bool)`, `nest 1` poses
-- `Tag (Wrap Int)`, and the matching set of each is the singleton naming the impl
-- of that name.  The body must dispatch on the dict its CALLER supplied, so the
-- two calls must select DIFFERENTLY: `boolW/intW`.
--
-- 🚨 THE FIXTURE IS SELF-EVIDENCING, WHICH IS WHY THE GENERAL IMPL IS ABSENT.
-- With no `impl Tag (Wrap a)` in the program there is no plausible-looking impl
-- for a mis-selection to land on: any answer other than `boolW/intW` names one
-- ground impl TWICE, for two calls at two different types.  MEASURED at the
-- pre-slice base `bffced42` on this tree: `boolW/boolW`, exit 0, on check, run
-- AND build alike -- the abstraction-time pick, applied to both instantiations.
--
-- 🚨 THE DEFECT HAD TWO HALVES AND EITHER ONE ALONE GRADES THIS ROW RED, which
-- is the reason it is one row and not two:
--   * CALL SIDE (`vectorGoal`, compiler/types/typecheck.mdk): a 1-ary argument
--     vector was declined, so the call site keyed its dict on the SLOT's mono
--     (`Bool`) instead of the predicate's own argument (`Wrap Bool`).
--   * CONSUMPTION SIDE (`registerSlotPredGivens`): the signature `=>` channel
--     never registered a PREDICATE-keyed given, so `entailAssum` could not answer
--     a structured goal and `entail` fell through to `entailInst`, statically
--     committing the body and leaving the caller's dict unread.
--   ⚠️ Landing the consumption side WITHOUT the call side is measurably WORSE
--   than base: the body then reads a dict keyed on the wrong mono, matches no
--   dispatcher arm and falls into LLVM `unreachable` (#1958).  If this row ever
--   needs re-deriving, that is the ordering it must not be re-derived into.
--
-- ⚠️ TWO `impl Tag` BLOCKS, so Section 4 derives a permutation pair -- and that
-- is a load-bearing part of this fixture, not a side effect.  At base the two
-- orders printed `boolW/boolW` and `intW/intW`: the winner was FIRST-DECLARED,
-- which DICT §3 forbids outright and which no value pin at a single declaration
-- order can see.  Section 4 is where that half is graded.
data Wrap a = Wrap a

interface Tag t where
  tagOf : t -> String

impl Tag (Wrap Bool) where
  tagOf _ = "boolW"

impl Tag (Wrap Int) where
  tagOf _ = "intW"

nest : Tag (Wrap a) => a -> String
nest x = tagOf (Wrap x)

main = println "\{nest True}/\{nest 1}"
